#: ../../legacy/emulators.rst:6
msgid "Managing Emulators"
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/emulators.rst:8
msgid ""
"The EaaSI platform relies on a number of open-source emulator projects to"
" perform the emulation aspect of software and environment management. "
"Replicating environments, troubleshooting, and expanding a node's "
"functionality all may involve some management of the emulators "
"themselves."
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/emulators.rst:14
msgid "\"Emulators\" in the EaaSI Interface"
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/emulators.rst:16
msgid ""
"Available emulators can be reviewed and managed via the \"Emulators\" tab"
" in the navigation menu."
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/emulators.rst:22
msgid ""
"\"Available emulators\" refers to emulation applications that have been "
"containerized and made easily available to the EaaSI network by the "
"development team via the OpenSLX team's `GitLab repo <https://gitlab.com"
"/emulation-as-a-service/emulators>`_. This does **not** mean they are "
"necessarily available for immediate use in the current node installation."
" The presence/availability of the emulator in the node is determined by "
"the \"Number of images\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/emulators.rst:27
msgid ""
"For instance, in the above screenshot, the example node contains QEMU "
"(two versions/images), BasiliskII and SheepShaver."
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/emulators.rst:29
msgid ""
"Details for each emulator - version number(s), Docker source info, etc. -"
" can be reviewed by clicking the \"Details\" button."
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/emulators.rst:36
msgid ""
"Again, in the above example, the node installation has two "
"images/versions of QEMU available: 2.12 and 3.1. This can be convenient "
"for troubleshooting environments, as certain versions of an emulator may "
"be more compatible with certain legacy operating systems or hardware than"
" others."
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/emulators.rst:40
msgid ""
"If an emulator has multiple images/versions in a node, the version that "
"EaaSI uses to run an environment can be viewed and edited on any given "
"environment details page under \"Environment Settings\":"
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/emulators.rst:46
msgid ""
"The \"Latest\" tag should be read as \"default\". You can tag a specific "
"image/version to be the default version offered when creating and running"
" environments using that emulator by clicking the \"Make Latest\" button "
"in the emulator detail page."
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/emulators.rst:50
msgid ""
"The \"latest\" (most recent available) version of an emulator is "
"generally the EaaSI team's *recommended* default, to allow for the most "
"up-to-date feature and security updates from these outside projects!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/emulators.rst:55
msgid "Emulator Templates"
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/emulators.rst:57
msgid ""
"Emulator images contain the template files used to assist with "
":term:`Hardware Configuration`. These templates provide common system "
"configurations for recommended emulators, and are visible in the UI when "
"either creating or importing a new environment:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/emulators.rst:63
msgid ""
"In the above image, for instance, the configurations for \"Generic 90s "
"PC\" and \"Generic 2000s PC\" are defined in templates contained in an "
"imported QEMU image; the \"Apple (Motorola)(7.x-8.x)\" was provided with "
"a BasiliskII container."
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/emulators.rst:66
msgid ""
"To start a new environment from scratch rather than replicating and "
"deriving from a remote node, these templates *must* be present, to "
"populate these menus in the UI. The target emulator must thus be imported"
" first (see below)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/emulators.rst:73
msgid "Adding New Emulator Containers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/emulators.rst:75
msgid ""
"New emulator containers can be added to the node in two ways: either "
"through environment replication or manual adding of Docker images."
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/emulators.rst:78
msgid ""
"Emulator images are automatically imported into a node if a node user "
"attempts to replicate an environment from a remote node in the network, "
"and the host node does not have the emulator image the remote node used "
"to originally create and configure that environment."
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/emulators.rst:82
msgid ""
"For example: a user at Node A sees a remote Mac OS 9.0.4 environment "
"configured using SheepShaver available from Node B. Node A does not "
"currently have a SheepShaver image installed. But, when the user chooses "
"to replicate, the appropriate SheepShaver image will be imported as part "
"of the environment replication process, with no extra input needed. "
"SheepShaver will now be available in Node A for future environment "
"creation and configuration as well."
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/emulators.rst:87
msgid ""
"The above method requires little to no direct management from the EaaSI "
"user. Node admins can also manually add emulator images as well by "
"clicking the \"Import Emulator\" button on the Emulators page:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/emulators.rst:92
msgid "This will bring up the Import Emulator pop-up menu:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/emulators.rst:96
msgid ""
"Instructions for filling out the Name and Tag fields diverge slightly "
"depending whether the user is attempting to import a new QEMU image, or "
"any other emulator container:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/emulators.rst:99
msgid ""
"**QEMU**: EaaS QEMU images are pushed to DockerHub. For the \"Name\" "
"field, fill in \"eaas/qemu-eaas\", then use the list of available tags on"
" DockerHub to specify the desired QEMU container: "
"`<https://hub.docker.com/r/eaas/qemu-eaas/tags/>`_."
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/emulators.rst:102
msgid ""
"**All others**: All other emulator images are hosted directly on GitLab. "
"To obtain the reference information for a Docker image, first choose the "
"appropriate emulator from the list of available projects on the EaaS "
"GitLab: `<https://gitlab.com/emulation-as-a-service/emulators>`_"
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/emulators.rst:106
msgid ""
"Once inside the relevant emulator project (e.g. `<https://gitlab.com"
"/emulation-as-a-service/emulators/vice-eaas>`_), click \"Registry\" on "
"the left-hand side GitLab menu. From this screen, you can find the link "
"to the containerized emulator (**use the full registy.gitlab.com address "
"as the \"Name\" of the emulator in the Import Emulator menu**) and "
"specific tagged versions, if desired."
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/emulators.rst:114
msgid ""
"If no tag is specified, the EaaSI import function will default to the "
"\"latest\" image as specified in each emulator's Dockerfile."
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/emulators.rst:117
msgid ""
"The optional \"alias\" field will populate the \"User version\" "
"information in the emulator details page and helps to differentiate when "
"there are multiple images for any one emulator in a node."
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/emulators.rst:120
msgid ""
"When Name, Tag, and Emulator alias fields have all been filled out as "
"desired, click the \"Import Emulator\" button. After a minute or two, the"
" new emulator image should be visible in the Emulators menu and available"
" for creating and configuring environments."
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:5
msgid "Navigating and Using the Legacy Interface"
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:7
msgid ""
"These guidelines and screenshots refer to the EaaSI Dev UI, as of April "
"2020. The Dev UI is intended for legacy support and gradual introduction "
"of cutting-edge features into the EaaSI stack, so the contents may not "
"reflect user experience. Please contact the EaaSI team for support if "
"needed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:13
msgid "Logging In"
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:15
msgid ""
"To log in to the EaaSI beta interface, navigate to the URL and provide "
"the login credentials provided by your node's system administrators."
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:22
msgid "Landing Page"
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:24
msgid ""
"The Dev UI landing page features live statistics regarding the health of "
"the local EaaSI node and the network as a whole (i.e. CPU and memory "
"usage). These may assist Admin users and systems administrators with "
"troubleshooting."
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:29
msgid ""
"The EaaSI navigation sidebar allows users to investigate and interact "
"with EaaSI's three primary types of :term:`resources <resource>` : "
"Environments_, Software_ and Objects_."
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:34
msgid ""
"The sidebar can also be used to access various pages for :ref:`importing "
"resources <add_resources_dev>`, :ref:`adding emulators "
"<managing_emulators_dev>`, and :ref:`adminstrative settings "
"<administration_dev>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:44
msgid "Environments"
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:47 ../../legacy/interface.rst:251
#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:372
msgid "Overview page"
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:49
msgid "Environments are sorted into tabs and sub-sections depending on:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:51
msgid "Derivative status (i.e. if it is a Base or Object Environment)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:52
msgid "Published status (its visibility to other users in the network)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:56
msgid ""
"\"Vanilla\" operating system environments have typically been marked as a"
" \"Base\" in their name. The notation \"V#\" has been used internally by "
"EaaSI staff to mark \"version\" number for base environments - e.g. "
"\"V1\" for the first attempt at importing a base image, \"V2\" for the "
"second attempt at importing a base image of the same system, etc. These "
"are usually the starting points for importing Software and building "
"derivative environment chains."
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:61
msgid ""
"Derivative environments can typically be identified by a name that "
"connotes its original base environment and the name of the Software added"
" (e.g. \"Windows 98 SE + Microsoft Office 97\")."
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:64
msgid ""
"The \"Base Environments\" tab is further broken down into three sub-"
"sections: *Private*, *Public* and *Remote*."
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:66
msgid ""
"*Private* environments are visible only to the currently logged-in node "
"user (see `Logging In`_). They are stored locally at the node."
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:68
msgid ""
"*Public* environments are visible and available for replication to all "
"users on the network. They are stored locally at the source node. (Users "
"at other nodes will see your *Public* environments in their *Remote* sub-"
"section)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:70
msgid ""
"*Remote* environments are all :ref:`published <publishing_dev>` "
"environments from other nodes on the network. These environments are not "
"stored locally but are available for replication to local storage (at "
"which point they will move to the *Public* sub-section)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:74
msgid ""
"The \"Object Environments\" tab contains only derivative :term:`Object "
"Environments <Content Environment>`. The environments displayed here "
"should **always** be unique to the node; no node should be able to see or"
" interact with other nodes' Object Environments."
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:80
msgid "For each tab and sub-section, the environments display table shows:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:82
msgid "*Name*: a descriptive human-readable name for the environment"
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:83
msgid ""
"*ID*: a unique UUID assigned to the environment to allow for sharing and "
"consistency across the network"
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:84
msgid ""
"*Owner*: will indicate ownership status of the environment within one "
"node when multi-user login functionality is added to the platform; for "
"now, all environments should list \"shared\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:86
msgid ""
"*Actions*: dropdown menu allowing for user and administrative actions "
"related to that environment"
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:90
msgid "Running environments"
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:91
msgid ""
"To run a selected environment in emulation, choose the \"Run "
"Environment\" option from the Actions dropdown menu."
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:95
msgid "Your emulation session should load immediately."
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:99
msgid ""
"Clicking on the emulation window will capture your mouse and keyboard "
"input to the emulated system. If Pointerlock is enabled, press \"Esc\" at"
" any point during the session to free your mouse to interact with your "
"host system."
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:102
msgid ""
"During the session, the EaaSI Action Menu will appear below the "
"navigation sidebar:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:109
msgid "Download Print Jobs"
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:108
msgid ""
"This button will appear contextually depending on whether \"Environment "
"can print\" has been selected in the environment record (see `Viewing and"
" editing environment records`_). If enabled, PostScript print jobs from "
"the emulated system should be converted and available here as "
"downloadable PDFs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:113
msgid "Change Media"
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:112
msgid ""
"This button will appear contextually depending on whether a multi-disk-"
"image Software or Digital Object has been loaded into the environment. "
"The user can switch between images within the emulation session, "
"mimicking the behavior of inserting and ejecting floppy disks or CD-ROMs "
"in physical drives."
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:117
msgid "Save Environment"
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:116
msgid ""
"Saves the current emulation session state as a new revision or derivative"
" environment; changes are isolated from the base image that began the "
"session. The guest operating system must be shut down before the "
"environment changes can be saved (see `Creating new environments`_)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:120
msgid "Send Ctrl-Alt-Del"
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:120
msgid ""
"Shortcut for sending the `\"Ctrl+Alt+Delete\" "
"<https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Control-Alt-Delete>`_ keyboard input to "
"the emulated operating system"
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:122
msgid "Send Esc"
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:123
msgid "Sends the \"Esc\" keyboard input to the emulated operating system"
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:125
msgid "Restart Session"
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:125
msgid ""
"Reloads the emulation session; any changes made to the environment during"
" the current session will be discarded"
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:129
msgid "Stop"
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:128
msgid ""
"Shuts down the emulation program and quits emulation session altogether; "
"any changes made to the environment during the session will be discarded"
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:131
msgid ""
"An emulation session can be ended in several ways: using the \"Stop\" "
"button from the Action Menu, shutting down the emulated operating system,"
" or simply navigating away from the page in your browser. In all "
"scenarios, the changes to the environment are not saved and running the "
"environment again will return the emulation session to its original "
"state."
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:137
msgid "Viewing and editing environment records"
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:139
msgid ""
"To view and edit a selected environment's metadata, choose the "
"\"Details\" option from the Actions dropdown menu in the environment "
"overview."
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:144
msgid ""
"An environment record will display several categories of "
"available/editable options:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:150
msgid "Description"
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:149
msgid ""
"allows for some brief description of the environment's content and "
"purpose. By default it is populated by the environment's most recent "
"save/revision message."
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:155
msgid "System"
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:153
msgid ""
"settings determine the :term:`Hardware Configuration`; users can see "
"which emulator (and version, if an emulator has multiple imported "
":ref:`containers <emulator_containers_dev>`) have been selected the run "
"the environment, as well as specific configuration options/arguments "
"passed to the underlying emulator."
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:161
msgid "Configured Drives"
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:158
msgid ""
"allow advanced users to determine which and how many drives (hard disk, "
"CD-ROM, floppy) are attached to the emulated machine, which may allow for"
" mounting multiple software/digital objects. This requires advanced "
"knowledge of the Hardware Configuration, compatible data binding "
"protocols and filesystems, etc. to properly fill out. Consult with the "
"EaaSI team if necessary."
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:164
msgid "UI Options"
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:164
msgid ""
"allow for more user-friendly, checkbox-style enabling of certain common "
"features:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:166
msgid ""
"*Environment can print* enables the EaaSI UI to intercept PostScript "
"printing jobs from the emulated operating system, convert them to PDF and"
" offer them to download from the host browser. Requires a compatible "
"PostScript printer driver to have been set up in the emulated operating "
"system."
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:170
msgid ""
"*Relative Mouse (Pointerlock)* allows the EaaSI interface to capture the "
"mouse for use of the emulated operating system only, if the user clicks "
"on the emulation window. (Press \"Esc\" at any time to free the mouse for"
" use in your host system/browser again)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:174
msgid ""
"*User file upload* is an experimental feature allowing end users to "
"import an arbitrary file from their host into the emulated machine. "
"Currently not functional in EaaSI installations."
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:177
msgid ""
"*XPRA Video (Experimental)* is an experimental feature for alternative "
"browser delivery of graphics/video. Not stable in EaaSI installations."
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:180
msgid ""
"*Requires clean shutdown* forces clean completion of an ACPI \"shutdown\""
" within the emulated operating system before the EaaSI Action Menu will "
"allow the user to save the environment. Can be enabled to encourage "
"healthy saving habits."
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:188
msgid "Networking"
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:185
msgid ""
"options are mostly included from OpenSLX's exploration into emulating "
"networked environments. EaaSI users will probably primarily be interested"
" in \"Enable networking\" and \"Enable internet access\" options to "
"connect emulated environments to the live web (requires an emulated "
"network adapter, and compatible hardware and TCP/IP drivers). For details"
" on EaaS's advanced server/network emulation, consult OpenSLX via the "
"EaaSI Tech Talk list."
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:191
msgid "Configured Software"
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:191
msgid ""
"lists any Software Objects that were loaded and saved into this "
"environment."
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:195
msgid "Revision History"
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:194
msgid ""
"describes the history of the environment in EaaSI, including any "
"revisions made to this environment *and* the environment(s) it was "
"derived from, if applicable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:197
msgid ""
"To edit settings and descriptive metadata, click the \"Edit\" button at "
"the top right corner of the page to open the record in editable mode."
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:204
msgid ""
"After making any changes to an environment record, be sure to click "
"\"Save\" at the bottom of the page to save your edits."
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:208
msgid ""
"An environment can also be run at any given time from its record page by "
"selecting the \"Run\" button at the top of the page."
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:212
msgid "Creating new environments"
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:214
msgid ""
"Adding new environments to the overview will depend on the type of "
"environment being created. See:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:216
msgid ":ref:`import_base_dev`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:217
msgid "`Creating a Derivative Environment with a Software Object`_"
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:218
msgid "`Creating an Object Environment`_"
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:222
msgid "Deleting environments"
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:225
msgid ""
"Only *Private* Base Environments and Object Environments can be deleted. "
"Any *Public* or *Remote* environments can not be deleted through the "
"interface, as doing so will disrupt functionality across the entire "
"network. See :ref:`publishing_dev`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:229
msgid ""
"Deleting even a local, private environment will likewise break any local,"
" private derivative environments that have been created from that "
"environment. Use caution whenever exercising this feature."
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:232
msgid ""
"Private environments can be deleted by node admin users in two locations "
"in the EaaSI interface: either through the Environments overview page, or"
" on any given environment's details page."
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:235
msgid ""
"To delete a private Base Environment from the Environments overview page,"
" select the \"Delete\" option from the Actions dropdown list for the "
"selected environment."
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:240
msgid ""
"To delete a private Base or Object Environment from its details page, "
"first select \"Details\" from the Actions dropdown list for the selected "
"environment, then click on the red \"Delete Environment\" button at the "
"bottom of the relevant details page."
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:248
msgid "Software"
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:253
msgid ""
"The Software overview page allows for browsing and adding Software "
"Objects within the node."
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:257
msgid ""
"*ID* and *Label* fields are currently the same descriptive value. Updates"
" will come with further front-end development."
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:259
msgid ""
"*Operating System* indicates whether the Software Object has been marked "
"as containing a bootable OS (e.g. is an operating system installation "
"disc)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:263
msgid "Viewing/editing records"
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:265
msgid ""
"Click the \"Edit\" button next to a selected Software Object to view its "
"metadata and details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:269
msgid ""
"Software metadata input is currently minimal. Free text fields allow for "
"input of license information and `WikiData QIDs "
"<https://www.wikidp.org/>`_ if desired or convenient for reference."
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:272
msgid ""
"You can also manually add PRONOM file format PUIDs to describe the "
"Software's rendering capabilities, if known/desired."
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:278
msgid "Adding Software"
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:280
msgid ""
"To add to the Software archive, software/installation media must first be"
" imported as an Object to the EaaSI platform using the "
":ref:`import_object_dev` process. The Object can then be labeled as "
"Software."
msgstr ""

#: ../../legacy/interface.rst:283
msgid ""
"To label an Object as Software, click the \"Add New Software\" button at "
"the top right of the Software overview page."
msgstr ""

